    {
      "id": "vfho3li",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1.6 Climate of Karnataka"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "p1v08r0",
      "content": "Karnataka has a tropical monsoon climate. The state experiences four distinct seasons:"
    },
    {
      "id": "ouk76er",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Season</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Period</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Temperature</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Key Features</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Winter (Cold Season)</p>",
            "<p>November – February</p>",
            "<p>15°C – 25°C</p>",
            "<p>Mild cold in plateau; cool nights in Malnad; light NE monsoon rains in coastal areas</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Summer (Hot Season)</p>",
            "<p>March – May</p>",
            "<p>28°C – 42°C</p>",
            "<p>Very hot in north Karnataka; Bidar, Raichur, Kalaburagi can exceed 42°C; pre-monsoon thunderstorms</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>South-West Monsoon</p>",
            "<p>June – September</p>",
            "<p>20°C – 30°C</p>",
            "<p>Main rainfall season; Coastal &amp; Malnad get 2,500–3,800 mm; plateau gets 600–900 mm; NE Karnataka least rainfall</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Retreating Monsoon</p>",
            "<p>October – November</p>",
            "<p>20°C – 28°C</p>",
            "<p>NE monsoon brings rain to SE Karnataka &amp; Tamil Nadu</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "g48jmxj",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Highest Rainfall District</strong></p>",
            "<p>Dakshina Kannada &amp; Uttara Kannada (coastal); Agumbe in Shivamogga gets ~7,000 mm (2nd rainiest in India)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Lowest Rainfall District</strong></p>",
            "<p>Bidar, Kalaburagi (less than 500 mm annually)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Agumbe</strong></p>",
            "<p>Called the 'Cherrapunji of South India'; in Malnad region, Shivamogga</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Soil Types</strong></p>",
            "<p>Red laterite (south), Black cotton (north-central Deccan), Alluvial (coastal), Sandy (coastal strip)</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
